1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,912
What does it take to engineer an AI
customer support platform that serves

2
00:00:04,932 --> 00:00:06,283
multiple businesses,

3
00:00:06,523 --> 00:00:08,002
while keeping their knowledge,

4
00:00:08,242 --> 00:00:09,324
conversations,

5
00:00:11,475 --> 00:00:13,299
and customer data isolated?

6
00:00:14,705 --> 00:00:18,586
This case study shows the product, the
architecture, what broke,

7
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:19,745
and what is unproven.

8
00:00:20,683 --> 00:00:24,288
Customers ask the same questions again and
again.

9
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:29,301
The answers usually exist, spread across
policy documents and web pages,

10
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:31,302
but someone has to find them each time.

11
00:00:31,542 --> 00:00:35,604
A support assistant grounded in a
business's own material can help.

12
00:00:36,947 --> 00:00:40,695
And if one shared platform serves many
separate businesses,

13
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:44,256
every answer has to come from the right
one, and only that one.

14
00:00:46,583 --> 00:00:50,687
Here is the real application, running
locally with fictional data.

15
00:00:50,707 --> 00:00:52,075
An owner signs in,

16
00:00:52,315 --> 00:00:54,691
and lands on the organization dashboard.

17
00:00:54,931 --> 00:00:58,859
A short wizard creates a chatbot, with its
behaviour and appearance.

18
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:05,427
Documents are uploaded, and a background
worker takes each one from queued,

19
00:01:05,447 --> 00:01:07,015
to processing, to ready.

20
00:01:07,255 --> 00:01:09,483
You can inspect the passages it produced,

21
00:01:09,722 --> 00:01:12,153
and test which ones a question retrieves.

22
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:14,823
Websites can be added too.

23
00:01:14,843 --> 00:01:18,150
Here, the crawler refuses a private
network address.

24
00:01:19,083 --> 00:01:23,078
In the playground, a question streams back
with a numbered source.

25
00:01:23,098 --> 00:01:24,616
One important note:

26
00:01:24,636 --> 00:01:27,516
this is a deterministic demonstration.

27
00:01:27,536 --> 00:01:31,441
The reply is a fixed sentence, not an AI
answer.

28
00:01:31,461 --> 00:01:35,238
What is real is the retrieval, the
streaming and the citation.

29
00:01:35,478 --> 00:01:38,465
An unrelated question gets a fallback
instead.

30
00:01:39,617 --> 00:01:43,277
Once the bot is active, the website's
origin is verified.

31
00:01:43,517 --> 00:01:47,721
A visitor asks through the embedded
widget, and the conversation is stored.

32
00:01:49,818 --> 00:01:52,870
The team sees it in the inbox and marks it
resolved.

33
00:01:54,614 --> 00:01:57,175
Analytics update from stored events.

34
00:01:57,195 --> 00:02:00,061
These figures are synthetic demonstration
data.

35
00:02:02,383 --> 00:02:04,747
The system is a modular monolith:

36
00:02:04,767 --> 00:02:09,784
one Python codebase and one database, run
as several processes.

37
00:02:09,804 --> 00:02:11,479
Think of it in four layers.

38
00:02:11,719 --> 00:02:13,670
First, experience.

39
00:02:13,690 --> 00:02:18,208
A dashboard built with Next.js, React and
TypeScript,

40
00:02:18,228 --> 00:02:22,102
and a small widget that opens an isolated
frame on a customer's site.

41
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:25,093
Second, the application.

42
00:02:25,113 --> 00:02:28,468
FastAPI handles ordinary requests.

43
00:02:28,488 --> 00:02:32,806
Dashboard requests are authenticated and
checked against the member's role,

44
00:02:32,826 --> 00:02:35,870
and chat answers stream back as server-
sent events.

45
00:02:36,817 --> 00:02:39,247
Third, data and processing.

46
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:44,497
PostgreSQL holds relational data, and
vectors with pgvector.

47
00:02:44,517 --> 00:02:48,059
Private object storage keeps the original
files.

48
00:02:48,079 --> 00:02:50,772
Redis carries the queue to Celery workers,

49
00:02:50,792 --> 00:02:54,997
and Celery Beat schedules a recovery sweep
for a separate maintenance worker.

50
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:57,692
Fourth, intelligence:

51
00:02:57,712 --> 00:03:01,379
document processing, retrieval and prompt
construction,

52
00:03:01,399 --> 00:03:04,654
with adapters for language and embedding
models.

53
00:03:04,674 --> 00:03:09,065
This is code inside those same processes,
not separate services.

54
00:03:10,017 --> 00:03:10,756
Together:

55
00:03:10,776 --> 00:03:15,843
synchronous requests, streamed answers,
and background jobs, in Docker containers.

56
00:03:16,083 --> 00:03:19,575
Several containers do not make this
microservices.

57
00:03:19,595 --> 00:03:22,439
This detailed diagram shows each
connection.

58
00:03:24,783 --> 00:03:29,851
Retrieval augmented generation, or RAG,
means finding evidence first,

59
00:03:29,871 --> 00:03:31,451
then writing from it.

60
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:35,015
A file or web page is extracted into text
and cleaned.

61
00:03:35,255 --> 00:03:39,153
It is split into overlapping chunks that
never cross a heading.

62
00:03:39,173 --> 00:03:43,641
Each chunk gets an embedding, a list of
numbers, stored beside the text.

63
00:03:44,583 --> 00:03:46,926
A question is searched two ways.

64
00:03:46,946 --> 00:03:51,026
Vector search compares embeddings, keyword
search matches words,

65
00:03:51,046 --> 00:03:53,238
and the two rankings are merged.

66
00:03:53,258 --> 00:03:57,537
Retrieval is always scoped to one
organization and one chatbot.

67
00:03:58,375 --> 00:04:01,251
The prompt is assembled, the model
streams,

68
00:04:01,271 --> 00:04:04,289
and citations are validated on the way
out.

69
00:04:04,309 --> 00:04:07,611
Only sources that actually entered the
prompt can be cited.

70
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:13,049
Citation provenance establishes which
sources were shown to the model.

71
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:15,960
It does not show that a source supports
the sentence.

72
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,344
Offline answer-bearing recall at five is
eighty-five percent,

73
00:04:20,364 --> 00:04:23,969
measured with stand-in embeddings that
only match words.

74
00:04:23,989 --> 00:04:26,219
The target is ninety percent.

75
00:04:26,239 --> 00:04:28,691
Real model accuracy is untested.

76
00:04:31,017 --> 00:04:34,666
Take two fictional businesses, each with
its own chatbot,

77
00:04:34,686 --> 00:04:36,465
documents and conversations.

78
00:04:36,705 --> 00:04:39,865
Here the second owner opens the first
one's address,

79
00:04:39,885 --> 00:04:41,925
and gets organization not found.

80
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:45,048
Several layers enforce this.

81
00:04:45,068 --> 00:04:47,411
Sessions are managed on the server.

82
00:04:47,431 --> 00:04:53,548
The browser holds only an opaque session
identifier, in an HttpOnly cookie.

83
00:04:53,568 --> 00:04:56,248
Roles decide what each member may do.

84
00:04:56,268 --> 00:04:58,698
Queries are scoped to the organization,

85
00:04:58,718 --> 00:05:03,361
and PostgreSQL enforces forced row-level
security underneath,

86
00:05:03,381 --> 00:05:05,985
so a missed filter is not enough to leak a
row.

87
00:05:06,917 --> 00:05:11,503
Public widgets receive short-lived tokens
limited to one conversation,

88
00:05:11,523 --> 00:05:14,478
and visitors get only the fields they
need.

89
00:05:14,498 --> 00:05:17,816
Forwarding headers are trusted only from
listed proxies,

90
00:05:17,836 --> 00:05:21,176
and AI providers must use HTTPS.

91
00:05:21,416 --> 00:05:25,248
This is tested design, not a security
certification,

92
00:05:25,268 --> 00:05:27,348
and not every attack has been tried.

93
00:05:29,683 --> 00:05:34,320
An audit then found problems that the
existing, passing tests had missed.

94
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:38,333
Here are eleven, some from the audit and
some found while fixing it.

95
00:05:39,533 --> 00:05:40,119
First.

96
00:05:40,139 --> 00:05:42,335
Ten simultaneous chats all failed.

97
00:05:42,575 --> 00:05:46,689
Each request held one database connection
while asking for another.

98
00:05:46,929 --> 00:05:50,589
The fix releases it early, keeps
transactions short,

99
00:05:50,609 --> 00:05:54,127
admits only bounded work, and makes
cleanup safe to repeat.

100
00:05:54,367 --> 00:05:58,296
At one hundred concurrent requests, sixty-
seven succeeded,

101
00:05:58,316 --> 00:06:01,059
thirty-three received a controlled
rejection,

102
00:06:01,079 --> 00:06:03,528
and there were zero unexpected failures.

103
00:06:03,768 --> 00:06:06,591
That used an offline provider on one
laptop.

104
00:06:07,567 --> 00:06:08,196
Second.

105
00:06:08,216 --> 00:06:10,651
A source cut from the prompt could still
be cited.

106
00:06:10,891 --> 00:06:14,754
Citations were checked against what was
selected, not what was shown.

107
00:06:14,994 --> 00:06:18,971
Now one renderer records exactly which
sources the model saw,

108
00:06:18,991 --> 00:06:20,158
even while streaming.

109
00:06:20,398 --> 00:06:24,121
The original failing test passes, with
thirty-two new tests.

110
00:06:24,361 --> 00:06:27,732
Whether a source supports a sentence is
still unverified.

111
00:06:28,683 --> 00:06:29,251
Third.

112
00:06:29,271 --> 00:06:33,640
Production settings accepted a plain HTTP
embedding endpoint.

113
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:37,163
No transport rule covered provider
addresses,

114
00:06:37,183 --> 00:06:40,112
so keys and documents could travel
unencrypted.

115
00:06:40,352 --> 00:06:45,297
Providers now require HTTPS with
certificate verification,

116
00:06:45,317 --> 00:06:48,609
refuse redirects, and ignore environment
proxies.

117
00:06:48,849 --> 00:06:52,180
One hundred and fifty-nine new transport
tests pass.

118
00:06:52,420 --> 00:06:55,531
No real external provider has been
exercised.

119
00:06:56,483 --> 00:06:57,100
Fourth.

120
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:00,682
A client could rewrite its own address and
avoid rate limits.

121
00:07:00,922 --> 00:07:04,070
The server trusted forwarding headers from
every peer.

122
00:07:04,310 --> 00:07:08,163
Now no proxy is trusted unless it is
explicitly listed.

123
00:07:08,402 --> 00:07:13,360
One hundred and ninety-seven unit cases
and seventeen runtime checks pass.

124
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,585
The real production ingress remains
unverified.

125
00:07:17,567 --> 00:07:18,172
Fifth.

126
00:07:18,192 --> 00:07:20,372
A hostile document could tie up a worker.

127
00:07:20,612 --> 00:07:24,167
Parsing ran inside the long-lived worker,
with no limits.

128
00:07:24,407 --> 00:07:28,936
Each parse now runs in a fresh process
with limits on time, processor,

129
00:07:28,956 --> 00:07:30,275
memory and output.

130
00:07:30,515 --> 00:07:34,818
One hundred new tests pass, including
killed and restarted workers.

131
00:07:35,058 --> 00:07:38,391
This is containment, not a complete
security sandbox.

132
00:07:39,367 --> 00:07:40,034
Sixth.

133
00:07:40,054 --> 00:07:43,546
A superseded job could resume and publish
over newer work.

134
00:07:43,786 --> 00:07:46,354
A claim did not say which attempt owned
the job.

135
00:07:46,594 --> 00:07:50,955
Every claim now carries an attempt number,
and stale attempts change nothing.

136
00:07:51,195 --> 00:07:55,907
Killed workers recovered in about thirty
seconds, using shortened test intervals.

137
00:07:56,147 --> 00:07:59,907
With default settings, recovery can take
about seventeen minutes.

138
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:03,371
Seventh, found while fixing that one.

139
00:08:03,391 --> 00:08:06,282
A stale crawl could overwrite newer stored
pages.

140
00:08:06,522 --> 00:08:08,582
Each page had one fixed storage key.

141
00:08:08,822 --> 00:08:10,814
Every write now gets a new key,

142
00:08:10,834 --> 00:08:14,277
published only by the attempt that still
owns the job.

143
00:08:14,297 --> 00:08:16,346
Unused uploads are reclaimed.

144
00:08:16,586 --> 00:08:19,794
Thirteen of twenty new tests failed before
the fix.

145
00:08:19,814 --> 00:08:21,163
All twenty pass now.

146
00:08:21,403 --> 00:08:24,075
It was tested on local object storage
only.

147
00:08:25,067 --> 00:08:25,723
Eighth.

148
00:08:25,743 --> 00:08:30,807
The inbox silently dropped older messages,
and a bad cursor returned page one.

149
00:08:31,047 --> 00:08:35,478
Cursors were unchecked, and screen state
survived an organization switch.

150
00:08:35,718 --> 00:08:40,509
Cursors are now scoped and validated,
older messages load on request,

151
00:08:40,529 --> 00:08:42,219
and screens reset safely.

152
00:08:42,459 --> 00:08:44,569
Twenty-six new backend tests pass.

153
00:08:44,809 --> 00:08:46,885
Pages are not a snapshot:

154
00:08:46,905 --> 00:08:49,863
a busy conversation can be missed until
refresh.

155
00:08:50,933 --> 00:08:51,800
Ninth.

156
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:55,612
Installing the widget twice broke it,
nothing could remove it,

157
00:08:55,632 --> 00:08:58,245
and visitors could see internal usage
fields.

158
00:08:58,485 --> 00:09:02,975
There was no instance registry, and the
dashboard response shape was reused.

159
00:09:03,215 --> 00:09:08,455
A registry now keeps one instance, destroy
cleans up, conversations resume,

160
00:09:08,475 --> 00:09:10,762
and public responses are allow-listed.

161
00:09:11,002 --> 00:09:15,639
The compressed loader is five thousand two
hundred and forty-eight bytes,

162
00:09:15,659 --> 00:09:18,678
against a budget of ten thousand two
hundred and forty.

163
00:09:18,918 --> 00:09:20,647
It was tested in Chrome only.

164
00:09:21,633 --> 00:09:22,187
Tenth.

165
00:09:22,207 --> 00:09:24,770
Recovery shared the busy ingestion
workers.

166
00:09:25,010 --> 00:09:28,548
And a healthy scheduler only meant that a
process was running.

167
00:09:28,788 --> 00:09:33,957
Recovery now has its own queue and worker,
and health means tasks are being accepted.

168
00:09:34,197 --> 00:09:39,007
With every ingestion slot busy, recovery
still ran in under two seconds,

169
00:09:39,027 --> 00:09:40,082
at a test interval.

170
00:09:40,322 --> 00:09:43,363
One earlier scheduler crash remains
unexplained.

171
00:09:44,367 --> 00:09:45,160
Eleventh.

172
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:47,835
A backup only counts once it has been
restored.

173
00:09:48,075 --> 00:09:52,211
The rehearsal found the restored database
had regained delete rights.

174
00:09:52,451 --> 00:09:57,027
That is fixed, and restore compares data
fingerprints, counts and permissions.

175
00:09:57,267 --> 00:09:58,919
The local rehearsal passed.

176
00:09:59,159 --> 00:10:04,015
Off-site copies, alerting and production-
scale recovery are not validated.

177
00:10:06,350 --> 00:10:08,322
The method was audit driven:

178
00:10:08,342 --> 00:10:12,897
build, measure a baseline, record
findings, and fix one at a time,

179
00:10:12,917 --> 00:10:15,186
with a failing test first where possible.

180
00:10:15,426 --> 00:10:19,966
Seven findings were remediated, with
stabilization checkpoints between them.

181
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:24,749
The first audit passed five hundred and
eighty-two backend tests,

182
00:10:24,769 --> 00:10:26,736
yet its added checks failed.

183
00:10:26,756 --> 00:10:29,027
All seven findings now pass locally.

184
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:36,224
The listed suites report twelve hundred
and nine backend tests,

185
00:10:36,244 --> 00:10:41,936
seventeen proxy tests run separately, two
hundred and sixty-two frontend unit tests,

186
00:10:41,956 --> 00:10:44,349
and seventeen browser journeys:

187
00:10:44,369 --> 00:10:45,909
fifteen hundred and five.

188
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:48,760
The path was not clean.

189
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:51,822
Earlier checkpoints kept failing tests on
record,

190
00:10:51,842 --> 00:10:54,323
and a latency gate was missed before it
held.

191
00:10:55,021 --> 00:10:59,642
To check the tests themselves, fixes were
deliberately broken ten times,

192
00:10:59,662 --> 00:11:01,102
and each break was caught.

193
00:11:02,050 --> 00:11:05,945
At one hundred concurrent chats, sixty-
seven succeeded.

194
00:11:05,965 --> 00:11:10,457
Thirty-three received a controlled
rejection, which is not an answer.

195
00:11:10,477 --> 00:11:12,895
None failed unexpectedly.

196
00:11:12,915 --> 00:11:18,195
Successful responses had a P95 of about
sixteen hundred and eighty-two

197
00:11:18,215 --> 00:11:19,119
milliseconds.

198
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:22,161
The gate passed ten of ten declared runs,

199
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:25,524
once with only twenty-five milliseconds to
spare.

200
00:11:25,544 --> 00:11:28,572
This is not real model or server
performance.

201
00:11:29,517 --> 00:11:34,793
The self-audit score moved from eighty and
a half to eighty-five point seven five.

202
00:11:34,813 --> 00:11:36,993
It is not independent certification.

203
00:11:37,233 --> 00:11:40,500
Hosted CI is configured, but has not been
run.

204
00:11:43,050 --> 00:11:48,276
This undergraduate portfolio project was
developed with AI coding assistants,

205
00:11:48,296 --> 00:11:50,438
including Codex and Claude Code.

206
00:11:50,678 --> 00:11:53,954
Its development process used written
requirements,

207
00:11:53,974 --> 00:11:57,692
explicit acceptance criteria, baseline
measurements,

208
00:11:57,712 --> 00:12:00,918
focused remediation and documented
validation.

209
00:12:01,158 --> 00:12:05,401
Failures were kept on record, and
decisions needing approval were held,

210
00:12:05,421 --> 00:12:06,380
not assumed.

211
00:12:08,950 --> 00:12:10,118
Where it stands:

212
00:12:10,138 --> 00:12:15,430
the core engineering is validated locally,
and all seven audit findings pass.

213
00:12:15,450 --> 00:12:18,680
A migration recovery policy still awaits
approval.

214
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:21,064
Real AI evaluation,

215
00:12:21,084 --> 00:12:25,426
semantic retrieval quality and answer
correctness are untested.

216
00:12:25,446 --> 00:12:29,076
Hosted CI and production deployment remain
open.

217
00:12:29,096 --> 00:12:31,101
The parser is not sandboxed,

218
00:12:31,121 --> 00:12:34,614
and production backup and monitoring are
unfinished.

219
00:12:34,634 --> 00:12:36,777
These are requirements still to meet.

220
00:12:37,717 --> 00:12:42,558
A multi-tenant AI customer support
platform with retrieval augmented

221
00:12:42,577 --> 00:12:43,389
generation.

222
00:12:43,629 --> 00:12:45,853
Its value is in the engineering:

223
00:12:45,873 --> 00:12:49,553
isolation, failure handling and evidence
you can check.

224
00:12:49,865 --> 00:12:51,261
Thank you for watching.

225
00:12:51,281 --> 00:12:53,312
The portfolio address is on screen.
